scope: |
  An instance declared `Monoid[Int]` does not satisfy a context bound `[A: Semigroup]`, even though
  `trait Monoid[A] extends Semigroup[A]`. Instance lookup compares the declared type EXACTLY and
  never walks the extends graph upward.

  REDUCED BEFORE CLAIMING, and the reduction is what stopped me building the wrong thing. The
  symptom is an ARITY refusal — `call to 'combineAllOption' passes 1 argument(s), it takes 2` — which
  reads like a defaulting or a currying bug. Probed:

      combineAll(List(7, 8, 9))         -> 24        `def combineAll[A: Monoid]`
      combineAllOption(List(7, 8, 9))   -> REFUSED   `def combineAllOption[A: Semigroup]`
      combineAllOption(xs) with `val xs: List[Int]` -> REFUSED, identically

  Same argument, same shape, same file. The declared TYPE ARGUMENT is not the difference and neither
  is the element type being invisible (§4a1's third edge, which is what I expected to find):
  `std/semigroup-monoid.ssc` declares `intSum`, `stringConcat` and `listConcat` ALL as `Monoid[…]`
  and NOT ONE as `Semigroup[…]`, so a `Semigroup` bound can never be satisfied by anything.

  TWO LOOKUP SITES, both exact: `resolveSummons` compares `typeHead(t) == head` (Lower.scala:1130)
  and `resolveGivenArgs` compares `it == concrete` (:1352).

  THE RULE IS ALREADY IN THIS FILE, one pass down: `rewriteGivenExtensionCalls` has `extendsFrom`
  and uses it to prefer the SUBTRAIT when several instances provide a method. This is the same graph
  read in the other direction — an instance of a subtrait SATISFIES a supertrait bound — so the fix
  is subsumption, not a new concept. It needs `traits` threaded into `resolveGivenArgs` and
  `resolveSummons`, which currently take only `(defs, objects)`.

  MEASUREMENT: N with floors DIFF 3 / CRASH 9. The histogram line `3 call to '…' passes 1
  argument(s), it takes 2` is the instrument; I do NOT yet know that all three are this cause, so
  the claim is for the rule and the yield is whatever it is — up to 3.
next: thread traits, subsume through extends at both sites, measure
